Nikola Milutinovic wrote:

>> I'm not sure what the standard says about CPP directives. I remember
>> reading that NewLines, Tabs and Spaces are treated as WhiteSpace and
>> that you can write the whole program in a single line, if you like.
>> Well, not with Digital, apparently. This problem is totaly the "feature"
>> of cpp.

The ANSI standard says that cpp directives can begin in any column -- so long
as the initiating # is the first non-whitespace character on the line.
Indentation is cool then -- so long as you preprocess the input in an
ANSI-compliant cpp mode.  Those modes include -std and -std1 but NOT -std0
which is the default.

>> This problem is totaly the "feature" of cpp.

Right.  -std0 mode is "K&R compatibility" mode and Kernighan and Ritchie
insisted that cpp directives begin in column 1.

One could argue that the K&R behavior is a feature, not a bug.  It allows
for faster preprocessing than the ANSI model (since the cpp can do a fast
scan down column 1 rather than have to consider whitespace).  It also improves
the cpp's ability to preprocess general text input (fortran, makefiles, etc.)
since #'s that aren't in column 1 are not an issue.

>> Since this feature of cpp persists in both 3.2 and in 4.0 versions, it
>> is highly unlikely we'll see it changed.

Right.  Too many users rely on the K&R behavior to change it.

>> Any feedback, sugestions and advice is welcome.

Sure.  Add -std to your command-line.  That'll change modes from "K&R" to
"relaxed ANSI" and your code work without modification.

Hope this helps.

\John Parks (Digital UNIX cpp author)







