Chris Chandler wrote:
> 
> Hello everyone,
> 
> I'm an NT administrator who just took over the admin for an
> Alpha box running OSF.  Over the Christmas break, the
> machine locked up. Knowing I was asking for trouble, I
> flicked the power switch.

This could have lead to: logical damage (file system corruption) or
physical damage (disk surface damage). Especially older DEC's drives
were easy to f*** up by power-out, I've had such cases. Low-level format
was able to repair the disk, but the data was lost.

> The closest thing I have to a backup is a couple of DAT's
> from 1995. I also do not seem to have any of the original
> media.

Judging by this, you haven't the faintest idea on that system's setup,
namely, the file system type and location. Also, "a couple of DAT's" can
be anything, from TAR arcive to AdvFS-vdump saveset. It would be
benefitial to you to find out what exactly on those DATs is.

Locate another DEC OSF machine with a DAT and see if any of the commands
recognize the DAT:

tar t
restore -t
vrestore -t
pax

> It passes the boot up tests, but "fails" when trying to load
> the OS.

If it wouldn't even start, you have a physical error. Anyway, you can't
boot from it and don't count on FS being repaired. You've lost root FS.

> The machine has an 500 MB internal and 4 Gig external Hard
> Drive.
> 
> The question is: All I care about is some data files and a
> small Perl application which I hope (but am not sure) reside
> on the external HD.

Most likely the external drive is OK. Judging by it's size, it's newer
and has automatic head parking on power-off. In other words, you can
access it, with some trouble.

> How do I access this data? Please be gentle, I'm fairly new
> to Unix.

If you are new to UNIX, better call in someone who is expirienced.
Anyway, your options are these.

1. Hook up CD-ROM (DEC's CD-ROM, be careful!).
   Boot from DEC OSF/1 installation CD.
   Choose "System management" ("System shell" on DU4.0)
   Now you have your machine booted with OSF/1 or UNIX.
   GOTO 3

2. Take that disk on another DEC OSF/1 station and hook it up.
   GOTO 3

3. Create /dev/rz* files for that disk.
     If that disk is dka100, then
     cd /dev
     ./MAKEDEV rz1
   Inspect the disk's label to see the partition layout.
     disklabel rz1
   This should have given you the insight on the FS's on that disk
   Mount the appropriate file system. Lets suppose c partition is our
aim.
     For UFS:
       mount /dev/rz1c /mnt
     For AdvFS:
       cd /etc ; mkdir fdmns ; cd fdmns ; mkdir temp_dom ; cd temp_dom
       ln -s /dev/rz1c
       Now you have to find out the file-set name
         showfsets temp_dom
         ..................
         Suppose it's name is "data".
       mount -t advfs temp_dom#data /mnt

Now you have that disk mounted. What will you do now? If you mounted it
on your machine, you're in single user mode, so, no network. Use tape.

Nix.



